<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Using limit Rules</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Suppose you have the following limit expressions:</p>
<p>\( \lim_{{x \to {@limit_point@}}} f(x) = {@f(x)@} \) and \( \lim_{{x \to {@limit_point@}}} g(x) =  {@g(x)@}  \)</p>
<p>
\( \displaystyle
\begin{align*} 
\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} \frac{{@coef1@}f(x)-g(x)}{\left(f(x)+{@const@}\right)^{{@pwr@}}} &=\frac{\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} \left( {@coef1@}f(x)-g(x)\right )}{\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}}\left(f(x)+{@const@}\right)^{{@pwr@}}} \qquad \qquad \qquad (a)\\
  & \\
      &=\frac{\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} {@coef1@}f(x)- \lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} g(x)}{\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}}\left(f(x)+{@const@}\right)^{{@pwr@}}} \qquad \qquad (b)\\
  & \\
      &=\frac{\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} {@coef1@}f(x)- \lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} g(x)}{\left( \lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}}f(x)+\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}}{@const@}\right)^{{@pwr@}}}  \qquad \qquad (c)\\
  & \\
      %&=\frac{{@coef1@}({@a1@})-({@a2@})}{\left(({@a1@}+{@const@})\right)^{{@pwr@}}}  \\
  & \\
      %&={@ta1@}
\end{align*}
\)
 \(\qquad \qquad \qquad \quad  \quad=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<ol>
<li>In step \((a)\), what rule or property allows us to separate these limits into two separate limits in the numerator and denominator?[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</li>
<li>What mathematical rule or theorem justifies this separation of limits in step \((b)\)?[[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</li>
<li>What mathematical properties are applied in \((c)\)?[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</li>
</ol>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>From the given limit expressions we can find the 
\( \displaystyle \lim_{{x \to {@limit_point@}}} \frac{{@coef1@}f(x)-g(x)}{\left(f(x)+{@const@}\right)^{{@pwr@}}}\) </p>
<p>\( \lim_{{x \to {@limit_point@}}} f(x) = {@f(x)@} \) and \( \lim_{{x \to {@limit_point@}}} g(x) =  {@g(x)@}  \)</p>
<p>
\( \displaystyle
\begin{align*} 
\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} \frac{{@coef1@}f(x)-g(x)}{\left(f(x)+{@const@}\right)^{{@pwr@}}} &=\frac{\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} \left( {@coef1@}f(x)-g(x)\right )}{\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}}\left(f(x)+{@const@}\right)^{{@pwr@}}} \qquad \qquad \qquad (a)\\
      &=\frac{\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} {@coef1@}f(x)- \lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} g(x)}{\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}}\left(f(x)+{@const@}\right)^{{@pwr@}}} \qquad \qquad (b)\\
      &=\frac{\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} {@coef1@}f(x)- \lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}} g(x)}{\left( \lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}}f(x)+\lim_{x \to {@limit_point@}}{@const@}\right)^{{@pwr@}}}  \qquad \qquad (c)\\
      &=\frac{{@coef1@}({@a1@})-({@a2@})}{\left(({@a1@}+{@const@})\right)^{{@pwr@}}}  \\
      &={@ta1@}
\end{align*}
\)

<ol>
<li>
<p>In step \((a)\) of the calculation, we separated the limit expression using a specific rule. The correct mathematical rule used in step \((a)\) is: </p>
<p><strong>{@mcq_correct(ta2)[1]@}:</strong> \( \displaystyle \lim_{{x \to a}} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \frac{\lim_{{x \to a}} f(x)}{\lim_{{x \to a}} g(x)} \)</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>In step \((b)\) the rule or principle that justifies this separation of limits in step \((b)\) is:</p>
<p><strong>{@mcq_correct(ta3)[1]@}:</strong> \( \displaystyle  \lim_{{x \to a}} [f(x) \pm g(x)] = \lim_{{x \to a}} f(x) \pm \lim_{{x \to a}} g(x) \)</p>
</li>

<li>
<p>In step \((c)\) of the calculation, we separated the limits for constants and functions. The mathematical property that allows us to do this separation in step \((c)\) are:</p>
<p><strong> Limit of a Constant, The Sum and Difference Rule & Power rule for Limits.</strong></p>
</li>
</ol>]]></text>
